Bot Protection is a security mechanism that detects and blocks automated programs (bots) from abusing your website, mobile app, or API while allowing
legitimate users and trusted bots (like search engine crawlers) to access the application.

Why do we need Bot Protection?

Without bot protection, attackers can use bots to:

Brute-force login (trying thousands of passwords)
Credential stuffing (using leaked usernames/passwords)
Web scraping (stealing your website's data)
Spam (creating fake accounts or posting spam)
Scalping (buying tickets or products automatically)
DDoS/HTTP Flood attacks (sending massive requests to slow or crash your application)

How Bot Protection Works

A bot protection solution usually combines multiple techniques:

Rate Limiting
Limits the number of requests from an IP or user.
Example: Maximum 100 requests per minute.

CAPTCHA
Asks users to verify they are human when suspicious activity is detected.

Behavior Analysis
Detects non-human behavior such as:
Extremely fast clicks
Repeated requests
No mouse movement
Unnatural browsing patterns

Device/Browser Fingerprinting
Identifies devices using browser and system characteristics.

IP Reputation
Blocks requests from known malicious IP addresses.

WAF (Web Application Firewall)
Filters malicious requests before they reach your application.

Machine Learning
Learns normal user behavior and detects anomalies automatically.


Example

Suppose your login API is:

POST /api/login

A normal user:

1 login request every few minutes

A bot:

500 login requests in 30 seconds


Bot protection can:

Block the IP
Require a CAPTCHA
Temporarily throttle requests
Alert administrators

Bot Protection in Spring Boot

If you're building a Spring Boot application, common approaches include:

Spring Security for authentication and authorization.
Rate limiting using libraries like Bucket4j or Resilience4j.
CAPTCHA integration (Google reCAPTCHA or Cloudflare Turnstile).
Reverse proxy/WAF such as Cloudflare, NGINX, or AWS WAF.
Logging and monitoring with tools like ELK or Prometheus to detect unusual traffic patterns.


Interview Answer (30 seconds)

Bot protection is a security mechanism that prevents automated programs from abusing web applications and APIs.
It protects against attacks like credential stuffing, brute-force logins, scraping, spam, and DDoS.
Common techniques include rate limiting, CAPTCHA, IP reputation, device fingerprinting, behavior analysis, WAFs, and machine learning to distinguish
legitimate users from malicious bots.